\section{Experiments}

All experiments use open-weight 7--9B parameter models evaluated with vLLM~0.9.2 on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96GB) with binary correctness scoring.

\subsection{Single-Model Baseline}
\label{sec:single_model}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_single_model_variance}
\caption{Single-model variance decomposition (Llama-3.1-8B, MMLU). Item difficulty dominates (58.4\%); all main facet effects are $<$1\%. Item-conditional interactions total 17.2\%.}
\label{fig:single_model_var}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Design.} The G-study crosses six facets: 200 items $\times$ 3 numerical precisions (BF16, FP16, FP32) $\times$ 6 prompt templates $\times$ 6 random seeds $\times$ 3 temperature levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) $\times$ 4 answer orderings, yielding 259,200 binary observations. Variance components are estimated via Henderson Method~I with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples).

\paragraph{Results.} Item difficulty dominates at 58.4\%, with residual at 23.1\% (Figure~\ref{fig:single_model_var}). All main facet effects are negligible ($<$1\% each); the interesting structure is in item-conditional interactions (17.2\% total), where prompt$\times$item (5.5\%) is largest. The generalizability coefficient $G_\text{item} = 0.936$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:gcoeff}); D-study projection (Eq.~\ref{eq:dstudy}) gives $G \geq 0.80$ with 55 items.

For text-exact-match scoring, temperature dominates at 79.7\% and $G_\text{item}$ collapses to 0.007, motivating our exclusive use of binary correctness.

\subsection{Cross-Model Variance}

Extending to eight models (BF16 subset, 460,800 records), Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} shows that item difficulty drops from 58.4\% to 37.9\%, while model$\times$item interaction emerges at 30.2\% (up to 36\% across benchmarks, \S\ref{sec:format_conditional}). The model main effect is 3--5\% (MC) and 9--11\% (FF)\footnote{Ranges rounded to nearest integer; exact values are 2.8--5.4\% (MC) and 8.5--10.7\% (FF) per Table~\ref{tab:cross_benchmark}.}, suggesting that models differ primarily in \emph{which items} they find difficult rather than in overall ability level. Figure~\ref{fig:item_heatmap} visualizes this pattern across models and benchmarks. Leave-one-model-out (LOMO) analysis (Table~\ref{tab:lomo_full}) confirms this structure is stable: no single model removal changes $G$ by more than 0.030 across all five benchmarks. Inter-model agreement independently quantifies this pattern: pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$ ranges from 0.326 (MATH) to 0.461 (MMLU), indicating only fair-to-moderate agreement on individual items despite similar aggregate accuracy---consistent with models differing in \emph{which items} they solve rather than in overall ability (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).

